                <pb facs="0689" n="599" sameAs="frus:frus1875v01/pg599"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Subinclosure 3 in No. 697.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Austin to Mr.
                            Loring.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Colonial Secretary’s Office</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Hong-Kong</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1874-09-02"><hi rendition="#italic">September</hi> 2,
                        1874</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 397.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your letter of the 28th ultimo, and to acquaint you in reply that inasmuch as
                    the case of Joseph D. Ellis has been submitted to the decision of the full
                    court, and the first step in the appeal has already been taken, it would, at
                    present, be premature to express any opinion in the matter. I have the honor to
                    be, sir, your most obedient servant,</p>
                <closer><signed>J. GARDINER AUSTIN,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Colonial
                            Secretary.</hi></signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">H. S. Loring</hi>, Esq.,<lb/><hi
                            rendition="#italic">Vice-Consul for the United States,
                        Song-Kong</hi>.</p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
